# LINCOA

LINCOA is a local derivative-free optimizer for smooth functions with linear
constraints. It builds a quadratic model from objective values, then searches
that model inside a trust region while accounting for the active constraints.
You supply the objective, any box bounds, and the matrices defining linear
equalities and inequalities.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/lincoa/struct.Lincoa.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                             |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A scalar function, preferably smooth                     |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction`; no derivatives                           |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                           |
  | Constraints | Bounds, linear equalities, and linear inequalities       |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`              |

## When to use it

Consider LINCOA when gradients are unavailable or expensive and the feasible
region is described by linear constraints. Its quadratic model can capture
curvature that a linear approximation misses. Powell's [2015
paper](#ref-powell-2015-lincoa) describes the constrained trust-region step.

For a problem with only box bounds, compare [BOBYQA](../bobyqa/). For an
unconstrained problem, compare [NEWUOA](../newuoa/). If any constraint is
nonlinear, use a method that supports that block, such as
[COBYLA](../cobyla/). When the objective is nonsmooth, compare
[MADS](../mads/) when its supported constraint modes fit the problem; it does
not accept LINCOA's general linear blocks.

LINCOA follows a local model, so the starting point matters. The objective
should also be defined outside the feasible region: interpolation and geometry
steps can sample infeasible points even though the published incumbent comes
from the feasible model points.

## How it works

LINCOA maintains an interpolation set and a quadratic objective model shared in
structure with NEWUOA and BOBYQA. Updating the model minimizes the change in its
Hessian in the Frobenius norm while satisfying the interpolation conditions.
This allows a quadratic model to use fewer points than a fully determined
quadratic would require.

A projected truncated conjugate-gradient method searches for a step that
reduces the model inside the trust region and the linear feasible region. An
active-set QR factorization tracks constraint normals that restrict the step.
Other iterations improve the interpolation geometry so the model remains
useful.

Basin converts every present constraint block to inequalities `A x <= b`.
Bounds contribute coordinate rows, equalities contribute two rows with
opposite signs, and each nonzero row is normalized. The resolution `rho`
decreases toward the configured final radius as the run progresses.

## Example

The unconstrained minimum of this quadratic is `[2.0, 2.0]`. Requiring
`x[0] + x[1] <= 2.0` moves the minimum to `[1.0, 1.0]`. The starting point is
feasible, and the constraint matrix uses Basin's pure-Rust `DenseMatrix` type.

<!-- compile: solver-lincoa -->
```rust
use basin::{
    Constrained, ConstraintSet, CostFunction, DenseMatrix, Executor, Lincoa,
    LinearBlock, Termination,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 2.0).powi(2) + (x[1] - 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let constraints = ConstraintSet::new().with_linear_inequalities(
        LinearBlock::new(
            DenseMatrix::from_row_slice(1, 2, &[1.0, 1.0]),
            vec![2.0],
        ),
    );
    let problem = Constrained::new(Quadratic, constraints);
    let solver = Lincoa::new()
        .with_initial_radius(0.5)
        .with_final_radius(1e-7);

    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![0.0, 0.0])
        .max_cost_evals(500)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    let x = result.best_param();
    assert!(x[0] + x[1] <= 2.0 + 1e-8);
    assert!((x[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-3);
    assert!((x[1] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-3);
}
```

`Constrained` attaches the declaration to the objective without requiring a
separate `Constraints` implementation. Add bounds or linear equalities to the
same `ConstraintSet` when the problem needs them. Nonlinear blocks are
unsupported and are rejected by the solver's type requirements.

## Practical considerations

### Start inside the intended feasible region

LINCOA does not project an infeasible starting point. It relaxes each offending
right-hand side to admit that point, following Powell's algorithm. This changes
the region being optimized, including any affected bounds or equalities.
Provide a feasible start and check the returned point against your original
constraints. Zero-normal rows are dropped, so validate degenerate rows before
passing them to the solver.

The objective must accept infeasible interpolation and geometry probes. Linear
constraints limit the accepted search point; they do not make every objective
evaluation safe for a function defined only on the feasible region.

### Scale the variables and model resolution

The initial radius defaults to `1.0` and the final radius to `1e-6`, with
`initial_radius > final_radius > 0`. Choose the first for a useful initial
change in your parameters and the second for the resolution your objective
evaluations can support. A small radius is a geometric stopping condition,
not a bound on the error in the parameters or objective.

The default interpolation set contains `2n + 1` points for `n` parameters.
`with_npt` changes that size. More points increase initialization evaluations
and model storage, so keep the default unless you have a reason to change it.

### Separate convergence from the evaluation budget

The algorithm stops naturally when its final-radius stage finishes.
`with_absolute_radius_tolerance` adds an optional earlier check on `rho`; it
does not change the radius schedule. Keep evaluation and iteration limits on
the executor and inspect the termination report. Radius convergence alone
does not establish feasibility for the original constraints or global
optimality.

## Implementation

LINCOA publishes the feasible model incumbent and objective through
`PointState`. The solver owns the interpolation model, active-set factors, and
radius schedule. Use `Executor::run_with_solver` to retain the solver and read
`rho()` or `geometry_diagnostics()`. The diagnostic gradient belongs to the
quadratic model, not the objective.

Fresh initialization rebuilds the model and reevaluates its interpolation
points. Preserve an `ExactCheckpoint` when continuing the same numerical run;
a parameter snapshot starts a new model.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. Present
linear blocks require matrix operations supplied by the corresponding dense
backends. Model calculations use internal pure-Rust buffers without a
BLAS/LAPACK provider and work on WASM. Basin cross-validates the implementation
against [PRIMA](https://github.com/libprima/prima).

